From f88d40c542e2ce2853674d08de554f1241ec3d47 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jjude80 <18168364+jjude80@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 00:27:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/9] fix(access): revoke the previous role's grants on
 demotion

updateMember seeded the new role's defaults on a role change but never withdrew
the old role's. Role defaults are materialized as ordinary grant rows, so a
demoted member kept them -- users:manage_permissions among them, which is a
self-escalation primitive: the demoted member can re-grant themselves exactly
what the demotion removed.

pruneSupersededRoleDefaultGrants deletes the previous role's defaults that the
new role does not confer, only where granted_by_user_id IS NULL, and runs inside
the membership transaction. A permission a human granted deliberately carries
that human's id and survives the demotion.

The NULL predicate is only sound if role defaults never record a granting human,
and three paths did: board-claim, company-create and company-clone all stamped
their owner's id onto owner defaults. Those rows then looked like explicit grants
and a later demotion could not withdraw them, so the escalation path stayed open
for exactly the highest-privilege principals -- reproduced before this change,
which kept both joins:approve and users:manage_permissions. ensureHumanRoleDefaultGrants
and ensureRoleDefaultGrants no longer accept a granting human, so role defaults
are recorded as role defaults and the mistake is a compile error.

Rows written before this change remain ambiguous: a row that is both a role
default and explicitly held is undecidable from the table alone, because
principal_permission_grants has no provenance column. Those rows keep their
current behaviour and need an owner decision before a backfill.

Verification (run here): 31/31 in access-service.test.ts and
access-routes-permissions-upgrade.test.ts, 190/190 across board-claim,
company-portability, portability-routes, cloud-floor, cross-company-authz,
companies-service and cloud-tenant-provisioning, 159/159 across the wider
access/membership surface. tsc --noEmit clean. With the service changes stashed,
the three demotion tests fail and 23 pre-existing assertions still pass.

