From 67772bd1d73d4897d262fad0c7513cb291147059 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jjude80 <18168364+jjude80@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 00:20:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] fix(access): record grant provenance instead of inferring
 it

Grant provenance was inferred from `granted_by_user_id IS NULL`, which is
wrong in both directions: board-claim, company-create and company-clone
seeded role defaults stamped with the owner's own id, so those rows read
as deliberate grants and a demotion kept `users:manage_permissions` for
the principals most likely to hold it.

Add `principal_permission_grants.granted_via_role`, written at insert time
by every path that materializes a role default, and point the demotion
prune at it.

`mergeRoleDefaultGrants` now returns which keys it contributed, so a caller
can tell a key it supplied from one the role added. A supplied key stays
deliberate even when the role also implies it: marking it role-derived
would let a demotion silently revoke a grant a person made on purpose.
`setPrincipalPermission` clears the marker for the same reason.

That resolves the case the schema could not previously decide -- a
permission that is both a role default and explicitly held. The writer
knows which list a key came from, so the two rows differ only in
provenance and the demotion makes the right call on each.

`insertMissingPrincipalGrants` takes `grantedViaRole` as a required field,
so leaving provenance unset is a type error rather than a silent
escalation path.

Rows predating the column carry a null marker and are treated as
deliberate, preserving them. The backfill policy for those is a decision,
not a mechanical detail, so `countUndecidableRoleDefaultGrants` returns
the affected rows instead of guessing at them.

Migration 0315. Access service 29/29, permissions/portability/tool-access
485/485, authorization 70/70; db, shared, server and ui typecheck clean.
Reverting the prune predicate alone fails the new ambiguous-case test, and
so does dropping the write-side marker -- both halves are load-bearing.

